WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:06.880
So with that, we have understood what are subqueries.

00:06.880 --> 00:10.360
And now you might ask me, why do we need them in the first place?

00:10.400 --> 00:12.160
Why subqueries are important.

00:12.200 --> 00:13.480
Let's have the following sketch.

00:13.520 --> 00:17.680
Now in our complex task, we might have to do several stuffs in our query.

00:17.680 --> 00:21.960
Like for example, the first step we have to go and join tables in order to prepare the data.

00:21.960 --> 00:24.720
And then the outcome of the joins should be filtered.

00:24.720 --> 00:26.320
So this is going to be our step two.

00:26.360 --> 00:32.120
And then on top of that in the step three we have to go and do transformations like maybe handling the

00:32.160 --> 00:35.280
nulls or creating new columns and many other stuff.

00:35.280 --> 00:40.520
And the last step we want to go and do that aggregations like summarizing the data or finding the average.

00:40.520 --> 00:45.600
Now if you go immediately and start writing the SQL query without having a plan, what can happen?

00:45.600 --> 00:51.720
You're going to end up having a long, complex SQL query, and it's going to be really hard to write

00:51.720 --> 00:53.640
and as well to understand and read.

00:53.640 --> 00:57.840
And now what we're going to do instead of that, we're going to go and divide our task based on those

00:57.840 --> 00:58.320
steps.

00:58.320 --> 01:01.320
So we're going to write one query section for each step.

01:01.320 --> 01:05.960
For example, for joining tables we're going to have one query for filtering, another one, transformation

01:06.000 --> 01:09.280
another one, and for the aggregation we're going to have the last query.

01:09.280 --> 01:15.680
So now since each step is like a preparation for the next step, we can go and say each of those queries

01:15.720 --> 01:16.920
is a subquery.

01:16.920 --> 01:21.200
So for step one, step two, step three we have subqueries.

01:21.200 --> 01:26.560
And they are all doing like calculations and preparations for the last step to the aggregations.

01:26.560 --> 01:28.920
And we call the last step the main query.

01:28.960 --> 01:32.200
And of course the whole thing can exist in one single query.

01:32.200 --> 01:38.120
So if you want to visualize this like you have a subquery in SQL and then this circle belongs to a bigger

01:38.120 --> 01:40.120
circle called the main query.

01:40.120 --> 01:44.800
By the way sometimes we call the main query as the outer query and the subquery.

01:44.800 --> 01:46.680
We can call it an inner query.

01:46.720 --> 01:52.320
And of course we can have many sub queries and many small circles inside each others to form something

01:52.320 --> 01:54.240
called nested queries.

01:54.240 --> 01:59.240
So this is the main purpose of using subqueries in our scripts and queries.

01:59.240 --> 02:03.680
It can help us to reduce the complexity, and it can make it easier to read.

02:03.680 --> 02:07.720
And we can have like a flow logical flow inside our queries.
